      "title": "Use separate API and redirect controllers",
      "rationale": "The API endpoints need JSON behavior while the root code endpoint needs redirect semantics, and separate mappings reduce accidental response-type coupling.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Put all endpoints in one controller",
        "Use a single catch-all controller with conditional response handling"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use URI parsing plus scheme and host checks for URL validation",
      "rationale": "This enforces absolute HTTP(S) destinations without attempting reachability checks, which are explicitly out of scope.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Use a regular expression",
        "Use a third-party URL validation library",
        "Allow any absolute URI and filter only by scheme"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Generate seven-character codes with SecureRandom and a URL-safe alphabet",
      "rationale": "This satisfies the fixed length, path safety, and resistance to sequential enumeration requirements while avoiding predictable identifiers.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Use database IDs encoded in base 62",
        "Use UUIDs truncated to seven characters",
        "Use sequential numeric codes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Perform click increments with an atomic repository update",
      "rationale": "A database-side increment prevents lost updates under concurrent follows and works with the required Spring Data JPA/H2 persistence model.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Read the entity, increment in Java, and save it",
        "Use synchronized application-level locking",
        "Use pessimistic row locking for every follow"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Construct shortUrl from the incoming request",
      "rationale": "The approved assumption avoids requiring deployment-specific configuration and preserves scheme, host, port, and context path for the caller.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Require a configured public base URL",
        "Return only the code and let clients construct the URL",
        "Hard-code localhost"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Return HTTP 302 Found for successful follows",
      "rationale": "This is the explicitly approved redirect behavior and communicates the original URL through the Location header.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Return 301 Moved Permanently",
        "Return 307 Temporary Redirect",
        "Return the destination URL as JSON"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Create a new persisted link for every submission",
      "rationale": "Repeated submissions are permitted to produce new codes, and the approved assumption specifies a distinct record and code for each successful request.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Reuse an existing code for the same original URL",
        "Reject duplicate original URLs"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "designDoc": "# URL Shortener Design\n\n## Architecture\n\nA single Spring Boot service in package `com.example.shortener` uses the existing application class and H2 configuration.\n\n- `LinkController`: handles link creation and link-detail API requests.\n- `RedirectController`: handles public short-code follows and returns redirects.\n- `LinkService`: coordinates validation, persistence, lookup, and click counting.\n- `UrlValidator`: validates absolute HTTP(S) URLs.\n- `ShortCodeGenerator`: generates cryptographically random seven-character codes.\n- `LinkRepository`: Spring Data JPA repository with lookup and atomic increment operations.\n- `GlobalExceptionHandler`: converts validation, malformed-request, not-found, and unexpected API errors to RFC 7807 `ProblemDetail` responses.\n- `Link`: JPA entity persisted in the configured H2 database.\n\nConstructor injection is used for every dependency. No in-memory link map or external infrastructure is introduced. All imports for persistence and validation use `jakarta.*` packages.\n\n## Data model\n\n`Link` is a JPA entity mapped to a `links` table:\n\n- `id`: generated `Long` primary key.\n- `url`: non-null original URL, stored as a sufficiently large `String` column.\n- `code`: non-null, unique `String` of exactly seven characters, with a database unique constraint and length limit of 7.\n- `clickCount`: non-null `long`, initialized to zero.\n\nThe entity does not expose mutable persistence details through API DTOs. API responses contain `url`, `code`, `shortUrl`, and `clickCount`.\n\nThe repository provides `Optional<Link> findByCode(String code)` and a modifying query equivalent to:\n\n`update Link l set l.clickCount = l.clickCount + 1 where l.code = :code`\n\nThe increment query is executed in a transaction and performs a database-level atomic increment, preventing lost updates during concurrent follows.\n\n## Request and response flow\n\n### Create\n\n1. `POST /api/links` receives `{ \"url\": \"https://example.com/path\" }`.\n2. Bean Validation rejects a missing or blank `url` at the controller boundary.\n3. `UrlValidator` parses the value as a URI and requires an absolute URI, a non-null host, and a scheme equal to `http` or `https`, case-insensitively. Relative URLs, malformed values, unsupported schemes, and values without a usable host are rejected.\n4. `LinkService` generates a new random code and persists a new entity with click count zero. Repeated submissions always create separate entities and codes.\n5. The controller constructs `shortUrl` from the incoming request scheme, server name, non-default port, context path, and code. Default HTTP port 80 and HTTPS port 443 are omitted; non-default ports are included.\n6. The service returns HTTP 201 with the stable JSON response schema.\n\nThe short URL uses the request's server-derived name rather than an independently configured public base URL. The implementation does not trust forwarded headers unless such proxy support is explicitly added later.\n\n### Details\n\n1. `GET /api/links/{code}` calls `LinkService` lookup only.\n2. Lookup does not execute the increment query.\n3. A known code returns HTTP 200 with the current click count. An unknown code produces HTTP 404 ProblemDetail.\n\n### Follow\n\n1. `GET /{code}` is mapped to one path segment matching the seven-character URL-safe code format, without intercepting `/api/links/...` mappings.\n2. `LinkService` loads the link. If absent, it throws a not-found exception and no update is performed.\n3. For a known link, the service executes the atomic increment query in a transaction and returns the original URL.\n4. The controller returns HTTP 302 Found with a `Location` header exactly equal to the stored original URL and no required response body.\n\nThere are no update or delete endpoints.\n\n## Code generation and collision handling\n\n`ShortCodeGenerator` uses `SecureRandom` and the URL-safe alphabet `A-Z`, `a-z`, `0-9`, `-`, `_`. It produces exactly seven characters. No database identifier, timestamp, counter, or predictable sequence is included.\n\nCreation retries up to a fixed implementation limit, such as 10 attempts:\n\n1. Generate a code.\n2. Optionally perform `existsByCode` as an inexpensive early collision check.\n3. Save and flush the entity so the database unique constraint is checked immediately.\n4. If a concurrent insert causes `DataIntegrityViolationException` for the unique code, retry with a newly generated code.\n5. If all attempts fail, throw an internal capacity exception mapped to HTTP 503 rather than returning a misleading client validation error.\n\nThe database unique constraint is authoritative; the existence check alone is not relied upon for correctness. Each save attempt runs in its own repository transaction because the service does not wrap the whole retry loop in one transaction, allowing a failed collision attempt to be safely retried.\n\n## Validation and error handling\n\nSuccessful API responses use JSON. Errors use RFC 7807 `ProblemDetail`, normally with `Content-Type: application/problem+json`, including at least `type`, `title`, `status`, and a non-empty `detail`; `instance` may be populated from the request URI.\n\n- Missing or blank URL: HTTP 400, detail identifies that `url` is required.\n- Malformed JSON or missing request body: HTTP 400 with a human-readable detail.\n- Malformed, relative, hostless, or non-HTTP(S) URL: HTTP 400 with a detail explaining that an absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL is required.\n- Unknown API or redirect code: HTTP 404 with a non-empty detail.\n- Exhausted code-generation retries: HTTP 503 with a generic service-availability detail and no sensitive information.\n- Unexpected server failures: HTTP 500 with a generic detail; internal exception messages and credentials are not exposed.\n\nNo credentials, API keys, passwords, or request secrets are logged or returned.\n\n## Concurrency and reliability\n\nThe click count is updated with `clickCount = clickCount + 1` in the database rather than by loading, incrementing, and saving an entity field. The follow service method is transactional, and the atomic update completes before the redirect response is returned. The service first confirms the code exists, so unknown codes do not create rows or increment anything.\n\nThe H2 database remains the configured in-memory database. `ddl-auto: update` and the existing datasource configuration are retained. Restart durability is intentionally not promised by the requirements.\n\nThe unique code constraint protects correctness under concurrent creation. The fixed retry limit prevents an infinite loop if the database becomes unavailable or code space is unexpectedly exhausted.\n\n## Backward compatibility and integration\n\nThis is a greenfield implementation. It retains `UrlShortenerApplication`, the existing base package, Gradle dependencies, H2 datasource, and JPA configuration. No existing endpoint or public class needs to be removed. The implementation uses only dependencies already available in the project and follows the required controller -> service -> repository layering.\n\nHTTP tests should use `@SpringBootTest` and `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` from `org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureMockMvc`, disable redirect following when asserting 302 behavior, and use the configured H2-backed application context. No `@DataJpaTest`, `TestRestTemplate`, `WebTestClient`, or obsolete `@MockBean` is required.\n\n## Rejected alternatives\n\n- In-memory `ConcurrentHashMap`: rejected because persistence through Spring Data JPA is required.\n- Sequential database IDs or counters as codes: rejected because codes must be difficult to guess.\n- UUID strings: rejected because the requirement calls for approximately seven characters and UUIDs are unnecessarily long.\n- Read-modify-write click updates: rejected because concurrent follows can lose increments.\n- Reusing an existing URL's code: rejected because every successful submission is allowed and designed to create a distinct link.\n- HTTP 301 or 307 redirects: rejected because the specified contract uses HTTP 302 Found.\n- External base-URL configuration: rejected because the stated assumption is to derive `shortUrl` from the incoming request and no additional infrastructure is needed.",
